        "original_id": "17593ecc-5053-5f46-a97f-5b9a1f6059b9",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Concepts: Catuaba, Guarani language\n- Products: Catuaba bark infusions, Brazilian drinks, herbal medicine\n- Places: Brazil, South America\n- Systems: Tupi–Guarani family, Tupian languages\n- Species: Erythroxylum vacciniifolium\n\nFacts:\n- Catuaba is a term used for infusions made from the bark of a number of trees native to Brazil.\n- The word Catuaba comes from Guarani and means “what gives strength to the Indian.”\n- Guarani is an indigenous language of South America.\n- Paraguayan Guarani is the primary variety of Guarani and has the endonym “avañe'ẽ,” meaning “the people's language.”\n- Guarani belongs to the Tupi–Guarani family of the Tupian languages.\n- Erythroxylum vacciniifolium is also known as Catuaba.\n- Erythroxylum vacciniifolium is used as an aphrodisiac in Brazilian drinks and herbal medicine.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
